From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HOTTY Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 10:46:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/25] hottyterm: a surface names a key from the bytes the
 program would read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A text field's keys are its program's keymap, named from what the
program would read for them (HOTTY SPEC §10.2, §10.4), so the surface
holding the keyboard gets each key as Ghostty encodes it, in the modes
the program set (hotty-blitz's hotty_host_key_bytes), not as a key code
and the text the layout composed. What a binding writes (text:, csi:,
esc:, cursor_key) goes to the surface first too: Ghostty's macOS Cmd+Left
writes 0x01, which a field reads as Control+a, and Option+Left writes
ESC b, Alt+b. The keys a field does not use reach the program as they
came; the press still decides where its release goes.

Upstream files: Surface.zig makes encodeKeyOpts pub and adds three
one-line hooks in performBindingAction.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
